#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084073} :activity/id #uuid "4c3d0ae4-f97f-57d8-9739-7d966b1a0f29" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049290 :topic/id #uuid "ea25b0e0-58d4-534d-a121-63a1222cdfb4" :topic/math-academy-id 712} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084522 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have an arithmetic sequence with first term $a_1 = 3$ and common difference $d = 2.$ This arithmetic sequence can be expressed recursively as follows: $$a_{n+1} = a_n + 2, \\qquad a_1 = 3, \\qquad n\\in\\mathbb N$$\n\nThis recursive formula generates the arithmetic sequence $3,5,7,9,\\ldots.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The recursive formula for a sequence is often called a **recurrence relation.**\n- This example is a **first-order recurrence relation** since we require only one previous term to calculate the next term.\n- We can use our knowledge of arithmetic sequences to express the $n$th term of this sequence explicitly as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ & =3+(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ & =2n+1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe can also prove that this explicit formula for $a_n$ is true using mathematical induction. First, let's recall the principle of induction." :tutorial/id #uuid "c05757f5-2e7d-5c7c-bf36-b4270041273a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9173 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "250f2f78-d3c7-5c7b-b531-8d578668b3e9" :step/math-academy-id 181631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084505 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following:\n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n- *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nLet's use the principle of induction to prove the result from the previous example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5003cdd-e1fc-50e6-af7c-3a160927f78a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7934 :tutorial/title "The Principle of Induction"} :step/id #uuid "e6e89d4a-5687-54f7-a7ea-cce29cc9d61f" :step/math-academy-id 181632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084075}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084521 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we saw that the first-order recurrence relation:\n\n$$a_{n+1} = a_n+2, \\qquad a_1 = 3, \\qquad n\\in\\mathbb N$$\n\nhas the explicit formula given by $$a_n = 2n+1, \\qquad n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$$\n\nLet's prove this result using mathematical induction.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$P(n): \\quad a_n = 2n+1, \\qquad n\\in\\mathbb N$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have $$a_1 = 3,$$ and for the right-hand side, we have $$2(1)+1 = 3.$$ Thus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k \\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq1,$ we have $$a_k = 2k+1.$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =2(k+1)+1 \\\\ & =2k+3.\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & ={a}_{k}+2.\\end{aligned}$$ By the inductive hypothesis $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}+2 & =(2k+1)+2 \\\\ & =2k+3,\\end{aligned}$$ as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, $a_n = 2n+1$ is true for all $n \\in\\mathbb{N}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5dc1436-8346-569b-b558-96d14f171e0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9172 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "927043e2-7a84-5975-b217-be86b02d9a60" :step/math-academy-id 181633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083196 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082928 :question/id #uuid "741e1b92-bacc-5ff8-9450-ae6942db4f3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13244"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6ea26-5bab-5804-9bf9-da3f91adaa7d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048631}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390307}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"} :step/id #uuid "b0dc1467-4194-5fa7-87ef-7ab3120ef003" :step/math-academy-id 181634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083197 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082930 :question/id #uuid "4a5c6254-3449-544b-abb6-fbb34019fc7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13246"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da3ef0ac-3908-573c-a1fd-4990a1822662" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418729}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Explicit Formulas for First-Order Recurrence Relations"} :step/id #uuid "2fa2ce65-3ee8-5db3-9607-b30654be030a" :step/math-academy-id 181637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084523 :tutorial/content "Now, consider the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$a_{n+2} = a_{n+1} + a_n, \\quad a_1 = 1,\\quad a_2 = 1, \\qquad n\\in\\mathbb N$$ This recurrence relation generates the Fibonacci sequence $1,1,2,3,5,8,13,\\ldots.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This is an example of a **second-order recurrence relation** since we require *two* previous terms to calculate the next term.\n- Deriving the explicit formula for the $n$th term is trickier here. However, it can be shown that $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[{(\\frac{1+\\sqrt{5}}{2})}^{n}-{(\\frac{1-\\sqrt{5}}{2})}^{n}].\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe can prove that our formula for $a_n$ is true using mathematical induction. However, this case differs from what we've seen previously because finding the next term in the sequence using the recursive formula requires knowledge of the previous *two* terms!\n\nTherefore, we need to employ a modified form of induction, known as **strong induction,** to prove that the explicit formula for a second-order recurrence relation is true. We discuss this next." :tutorial/id #uuid "c13baeec-5f49-5277-9576-375d69f477d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9174 :tutorial/title "Second-Order Recurrence Relations"} :step/id #uuid "242b772b-3b65-5f0f-accf-f303d0fe5156" :step/math-academy-id 181640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084513 :tutorial/content "The principle of **strong mathematical induction** states the following:\n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3), \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2), P(3),P(4),\\ldots,$ if required), and*\n- *$(P(1)\\land P(2)\\land \\ldots\\land P(k)) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb{N}.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$*\n\nThe principle of strong induction is equivalent to the principle of induction.\n\nStrong induction is useful when it's difficult to show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ because $P(k)$ alone does not give enough information to imply that $P(k+1)$ must be true.\n\nIn such cases, we can assume that *all* of the statements $P(1), P(2), \\ldots, P(k-1), P(k)$ are true, and then use *this* assumption to prove that $P(k+1)$ is true.\n\nLet's see how to prove the explicit formula for the $n$th term of a second-order recurrence relation using strong induction." :tutorial/id #uuid "d30d5ff1-2888-5a17-af2f-765156938ff7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8320 :tutorial/title "Strong Induction"} :step/id #uuid "b814a159-ff7e-5aaa-852f-ef093190f2c6" :step/math-academy-id 181641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084512 :tutorial/content "Given the recurrence relation $$a_{n+1} = 2a_{n} - a_{n -1}, \\qquad a_1 = 2, \\qquad a_2 = 3, \\qquad n\\in\\mathbb N, \\qquad n \\geq 2$$\n\nlet's use strong induction to prove the following statement: $$a_n = n+1, \\qquad \\forall n\\in\\mathbb N$$\n\nThe principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2), P(3),P(4),\\ldots,$ if required), and\n- $P(1), P(2), \\ldots, P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb{N}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$P(n): \\quad a_n = n+1$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula $a_{n+1} = 2a_{n}-a_{n-1}$ does not apply until $a_3,$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ For $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have $$a_1 = 2,$$ and for the right-hand side, we have $$(1)+1= 2.$$ Similarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have $$a_2 = 3,$$ and for the right-hand side, we have $$(2)+1= 3.$$ Thus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\leq k,$ where $k\\geq 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that $a_n=n+1$ for all $n\\leq k$ for some $k \\geq 2.$ In particular, we have the following: $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & =k+1 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & =k\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),\\ldots, P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that $$a_{k+1} = k+2 . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have $$\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =2{a}_{k}-{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}$$ By the inductive hypothesis $$\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{k}-{a}_{k-1} & =2(k+1)-(k) \\\\ & =2k+2-k \\\\ & =k+2,\\end{aligned}$$ as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, $a_n = n+1$ is true for all $n \\in\\mathbb{N}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3e4d95f-5244-57a4-9fce-c871d16c5d94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8271 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b15804b1-9487-5cf4-b531-6d581db8c1c6" :step/math-academy-id 181642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082929 :question/id #uuid "9d761b89-98e3-564b-a610-7ba670c215e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13245"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac5337bb-d568-5b4b-97df-c5b4595aa852" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050192}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418730}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"} :step/id #uuid "4c47308e-9397-5b8d-a27e-d9c6e416e9b2" :step/math-academy-id 181643 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082931 :question/id #uuid "d071b78e-e3a4-53c5-ac9c-83ee19aa9608" :question/math-academy-id "e-13247"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7acc0afa-d36d-538e-a5ee-b4cddad1375e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418731}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Explicit Formulas for Second-Order Recurrence Relations"} :step/id #uuid "78d03ea2-5c08-5e66-8bd4-7308f25e24a7" :step/math-academy-id 181646}] :activity/title "Strong Induction and Recurrence Relations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083416}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418733} :activity/id #uuid "6fb1db1b-09e6-5ab8-87d0-f199d1a691fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049290 :topic/id #uuid "ea25b0e0-58d4-534d-a121-63a1222cdfb4" :topic/math-academy-id 712} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da7b11d-6b7e-5cd3-bc5a-8eeb