answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418498} {:answer/id #uuid "5c83c54c-b7b7-5692-bb43-9350e8d99fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{k}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418549} {:answer/id #uuid "6777bc1a-e081-5161-803c-132c7eaba566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{k-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418556} {:answer/id #uuid "784b88ed-b88c-5cc8-a2e9-04a2bee94c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{k-1}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418574} :answer-field/id #uuid "dea67be1-b3be-5305-833f-b3fc4c3176fd" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b84a4f-fca5-53a5-86f4-b3e11aa6277f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418493} {:answer/id #uuid "59318786-554f-5074-b946-68acb1e309d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418545} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a8761b-4f4a-55f8-a1bd-8265cd963866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{8}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418646} :answer-field/id #uuid "edbab988-83a7-51b0-8b2b-9e2e344a1627" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90b99752-34a5-5bfa-9d99-9e9bd7bb0b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-183891" :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=59,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using strong induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all {{field-3}} for {{field-4}}. In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}={{field-5}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1}={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using strong induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={8}^{1}-5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={8}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base cases are true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side in both cases.\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all $n\\le k$ for $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={8}^{k}-5 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={8}^{k-1}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={8}^{k+1}-5.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$"} :step/id #uuid "161991d8-68d9-59a2-b87c-cd83a77e95e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "990507c8-8113-565b-a377-3722f691f05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418600} {:answer/id #uuid "e87eff46-132c-5c56-9839-6568821027e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418656} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7baba1a-7a51-5c24-b5a0-cd87cce015c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c3eec06-f9cd-59c2-a71f-8e9c59791f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{k+2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418548} {:answer/id #uuid "6b193165-0e4e-5d47-9d3e-f3dbc36991d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{k}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418559} {:answer/id #uuid "d61de6b2-184c-5f38-a158-0b4eb7629102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{k+1}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418643} :answer-field/id #uuid "0273d17f-dfc2-51b3-aac3-7d79b47b0da3" :answer-field/key "field-18" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418672} {:a