/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{k+2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418660} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4d8f6d-1525-5f18-9cf3-be8b0c47bdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{k}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418669} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb601b79-351a-52b0-b12a-40bd282111c0" :answer-field/key "field-11" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e91446-e233-56ae-8b2e-912db4cd8184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418494} {:answer/id #uuid "74cede6f-8565-59a5-aa74-b1f6b8a11cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418565} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa3fc45-33f4-5971-8ebc-511fc6a6a009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418605} {:answer/id #uuid "ee56fa75-31ae-51c5-bc7c-6f0a94f710a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418662} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7c458b-b260-5f49-a82b-0e33e12c3e4c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "626522ad-f0ef-5294-8f48-0e9468b2d95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183882" :question/problem "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}-5{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=21,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-8}} for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ for all {{field-9}} for some {{field-10}}. In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={{field-11}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =({{field-13}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{a}_{k}-5{a}_{k-1} & =6({{field-14}})-5({{field-15}}) \\\\  & =5({{field-16}})-{{field-17}} \\\\  & ={{field-18}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ is true for all {{field-19}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2),P(3),P(4),…,$ if required), and\n\n- $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}-5{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ \nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{1}-4=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=21,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{2}-4=21.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ for all $n\\le k$ for some $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={5}^{k}-4 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={5}^{k-1}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={5}^{k+1}-4.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =6{a}_{k}-5{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{a}_{k}-5{a}_{k-1} & =6({5}^{k}-4)-5({5}^{k-1}-4) \\\\  & =6({5}^{k})-6(4)-5({5}^{k-1})+5(4) \\\\  & =6({5}^{k})-24-{5}^{k}+20 \\\\  & =5({5}^{k})-4 \\\\  & ={5}^{k+1}-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-4$ is true for all $n\\in N.$"} :step/id #uuid "1a84aaf7-861e-578e-b1f0-5511f991547a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418732}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b2a43a2-78ab-5cc5-bb9f-b8a71a29ddc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{1}-{2}^{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418525} {:answer/id #uuid "7bde05bf-0ffc-5347-87b4-8a1aa7a6772b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5}^{1}-{2}^{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418575} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b35d91-a4c3-5b0e-a9c5-66e12a1e1cfb" :answer/typ