ce4-50ce-a3b5-301fb4761c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-183943" :question/problem "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=7{a}_{n}-10{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=21,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n},\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-8}} for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ for all {{field-9}} for some {{field-10}}. In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={{field-11}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =({{field-13}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1} & =7({{field-14}})-10({{field-15}}) \\\\  & =5({{field-16}})-2({{field-17}}) \\\\  & ={{field-18}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ is true for all {{field-19}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2),P(3),P(4),…,$ if required), and\n\n- $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=7{a}_{n}-10{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ \nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{1}-{2}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=21,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}=21.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\le k$ for some $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={5}^{k}-{2}^{k} \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={5}^{k-1}-{2}^{k-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={5}^{k+1}-{2}^{k+1}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{k}-10{a}_{k-1} & =7({5}^{k}-{2}^{k})-10({5}^{k-1}-{2}^{k-1}) \\\\  & =7({5}^{k})-7({2}^{k})-10({5}^{k-1})+10({2}^{k-1}) \\\\  & =7({5}^{k})-7({2}^{k})-2\\cdot 5({5}^{k-1})+5\\cdot 2({2}^{k-1}) \\\\  & =7({5}^{k})-7({2}^{k})-2({5}^{k})+5({2}^{k}) \\\\  & =5({5}^{k})-2({2}^{k}) \\\\  & ={5}^{k+1}-{2}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={5}^{n}-{2}^{n}$ is true for all $n\\in N.$"} :step/id #uuid "1da2ba32-5dd9-5645-83b8-9c97a88875cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3daf85f8-4c6b-5fce-a5d9-5d8e67006997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418528} {:answer/id #uuid "43fdc9c1-a461-5085-920d-409e55879a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{k}+{3}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418530} {:answer/id #uuid "93d18f87-b961-5baa-ae92-509f4d144ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{k-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418595} {:answer/id #uuid "b96a8275-d5ab-5d1c-b411-a6d0ee0cc524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418530} :answer-field/id #uuid "04554eb7-5cc8-5bc9-a21b-295be83f639f" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "677153fb-0bbe-5f53-b21f-2618c4f7db84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418555} {:answer/id #uuid "a04a76cb-7575-55e6-b1b1-5374a32a2e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418606} {:answer/id #uuid "ccefac2b-9381-5084-b5f5-7e390d6c195a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418632} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2fce83-84f5-5535-b3aa-9f347ad3951a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418663} :answer-field/id #uuid "04c8c8b3-b5f9