eld/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa4709c-0ed0-5575-8d78-490088c70437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418504} {:answer/id #uuid "809ec8b8-e30a-5d2a-b0ce-344135325e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418581} {:answer/id #uuid "9155e238-ca7f-5143-bded-a74a3780d197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{k}+{3}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418594} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a504ca-5938-5f28-9d17-636dea9efc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{k-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418504} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c858af-175b-5399-8a66-3bcb84e582ea" :answer-field/key "field-12" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1235af24-4bb5-5ea7-b9aa-86848a4558cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-183963" :question/problem "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n}-{3}^{n},\\,{a}_{1}=7,\\,n\\in N,\n$$\n\nuse mathematical induction to prove the following statement: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side of our statement, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}={{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k} & =4({{field-10}})-{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all {{field-13}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=7,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{4}^{1}+{3}^{1}=7.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={4}^{k}+{3}^{k}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{k}-{3}^{k} & =4({4}^{k}+{3}^{k})-{3}^{k} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {4}^{k}+4\\cdot {3}^{k}-{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}+{3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all $n\\in N.$"} :step/id #uuid "fe776bb0-9610-5892-93ae-ec019c23ffcd"}] :activity/title "Strong Induction and Recurrence Relations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418480}]}
